docs/SKILLS.md :: tighten :: Finalizes work by ensuring everything is committed, pushed to the remote, and reflected on GitHub: closes issues, posts summary comments, and presents a completion table. One gate for every stack: it detects the toolchains and frameworks itself (here `uv`, `npm` and `fastapi`) and runs each toolchain's checks — ruff check and format, an import check and pytest, then the declared `npm run lint:js` / `format:js:check` / `test:js`. Why each check runs as it does: the vendored `references/gate-topology.md`. Replaced `shipping-work-python-fastapi` (#240, gregoryfoster/skills#537).
docs/SKILLS.md :: tighten :: The skill is a whole-directory vendor symlink. The one project-local piece is the repo-root `scripts/pre-ship.sh`, a wrapper that execs the vendored gate through `skills/shipping-work/`. Step 1 resolves each script on its own, so a repo-root `scripts/pre-ship.sh` wins for `pre-ship.sh` alone and the other five steps still find theirs in the skill (gregoryfoster/skills#301). What it does: it **parses** `$PROJECT_ROOT/.env` line by line before delegating to the vendored gate, so the PostgreSQL tests see `TEST_DATABASE_URL` instead of silently skipping. It never *sources* that file — `source` executes it, and `.env` holds live PATs. In a linked worktree, which has no `.env`, it falls back to the primary checkout's and warns when neither exists; it exports the loaded names (never values) as `PRE_SHIP_ENV_NAMES`, which the vendored gate keys its pass stamp on, so a run with no env records no pass (#235, skills#497). Unlike upstream's recipe it never loads `/etc/wslcb-licensing-tracker/.env`: that holds the production `DATABASE_URL` (`tests/test_pre_ship_wrapper.py`). The stamp is recorded only for a tree with no untracked, unignored file, so the main checkout, which keeps `.skills/worktree_venv` untracked on purpose, never records one: every gate run there is a full run. pytest-xdist is not a dependency, so the suite runs serially (`.skills/test-workers` unset).
docs/SKILLS.md :: tighten :: Step 1.5's doc gate (`doc-check.sh`) is vendored, not forked: the sensitive-path list lives in `.skills/doc-sensitive-paths` at the repo root (#172). Entries match whole path *segments* at any depth, and the file replaces the script's defaults rather than extending them — 6 of the 12 upstream defaults matched nothing in this tree. `tests/test_doc_sensitive_paths.py` asserts every entry still matches a tracked file, so the list cannot go inert as the layout moves. A list where *no* entry matches anything makes the script exit 2 rather than print a clean green (gregoryfoster/skills#252).
docs/SKILLS.md :: tighten :: The advice printed on a hit — which doc sections to spot-check — lives beside it in `.skills/doc-sections`, one `<doc>: <sections>` line per doc (#173). It too replaces the defaults, whose two lines describe a generic FastAPI layout and never name `docs/SCHEMA.md`, `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md`, `docs/SKILLS.md` or `docs/STYLE.md`. Tailoring only one of the two files makes every hit end with a `Note: this project tailors …` line (gregoryfoster/skills#284). Upstream runs no dead-entry check on advice, so the same test file asserts each line names a tracked doc and drives the vendored script end to end to confirm a hit prints `(advice: .skills/doc-sections)`.
AGENTS.md :: tighten :: `shipping-work`'s Step 1.5 doc gate is vendored, not forked — tailor it by editing `.skills/doc-sensitive-paths` (segment-matched, replaces upstream's defaults; `tests/test_doc_sensitive_paths.py` keeps every entry live) (#172) and `.skills/doc-sections`, the advice printed on a hit (#173). Keep both files in step.
